    "body": "Chinese PV and solar equipment brand LEAPTING has taken the wraps off its new, battery-powered Auto-Navigation Forklift Robot (AFR) designed to speed up the installation of large, utility-scale solar power projects .\nThe newest member in a growing lineup of fully autonomous solar construction and maintenance robots , the latest LEAPTING AFR was revealed earlier this week at a solar technology conference in Dubai, where the heavy-duty, fully autonomous and battery-powered tracked forklift was called the “missing link” in solar material handling for its ability to delivers solar module pallets directly from terminals or laydown yards to installation rows without the need to put either a human operator or flagman in harm’s way.\n“The AFR combines GPS-RTK, laser-based simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM), and 3D computer vision to locate pallets, identify fork-hole coordinates in real time, and auto-plan insertion paths,” the company said, in a statement. “ It further features 360-degree multi-line LiDAR , intelligent obstacle avoidance, and a three-tier braking system.”\nThe thing, itself\nImage by LEAPTING.\nThe AFR uses a 48 V, 400 Ah lithium battery with a nominal capacity of 19.2 kWh, sending power to a pair of electric drive motors mounted high in the track. The combination is reportedly good for about 10 total hp and a full eight hours of continuous operation under “standard” operating conditions – more than enough, in other words, to handle a full day’s work.\nAdvertisement - scroll for more content\nAnd, thanks to its IP67 all-weather protection and fully tracked, tank-like mobility, the all-terrain LEAPTING forklift robot can handle 3,000 kg (~6,600 lbs.) of solar and PV materials per load across some of the most demanding job sites you can think, from desert sand and gravel to uneven, muddy, and icy ground, giving the company a product that could, conceivably, be deployed just about anywhere tomorrow.\nThe company claims the new AFR can automatically mount modules on trackers every 40 seconds (3–5x faster than manual labour) with placement accuracy under +/-1mm. You can check it out for yourself in the images, above, then let us know what you think of this newest solar construction robot in the comments.\nSOURCE | IMAGES : LEAPTING , via PV Magazine .\nIf you’re considering going solar, it’s always a good idea to get quotes from a few installers. To make sure you find a trusted, reliable solar installer near you that offers competitive pricing, check out EnergySage , a free service that makes it easy for you to go solar. It has hundreds of pre-vetted solar installers competing for your business, ensuring you get high-quality solutions and save 20-30% compared to going it alone. Plus, it’s free to use, and you won’t get sales calls until you select an installer and share your phone number with them.\nYour personalized solar quotes are easy to compare online and you’ll get access to unbiased Energy Advisors to help you every step of the way. Get started here .\nFTC: We use income earning auto affiliate links. More.\nStay up to date with the latest content by subscribing to Electrek on Google News . You’re reading Electrek— experts who break news about Tesla , electric vehicles, and green energy , day after day. Be sure to check out our homepage for all the latest news, and follow Electrek on Twitter , Facebook , and LinkedIn to stay in the loop. Don’t know where to start? Check out our YouTube channel for the latest reviews.",
